**Read:** [Aqueous corrosion — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Aqueous-Corrosion-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_3P6KCGYFDFBG1WYEJ2EZY6G7J5), reactions and potentials; [Corrosion current — Derek H. Lister](../sources/Corrosion-Current-Lister.lingua#r_13M9DHG16SHQ1WG0C46CADMMD5), coupled reactions. Follow the shared electron-balance and Nernst Records with the earlier chemical-potential account.

A thermodynamically favorable reaction may be slowed by an activation barrier, a protective film or supply of reactants. Conversely, a material that is stable against one reaction can fail by another. Write complete balanced reactions and identify the reservoir conditions before drawing conclusions from a standard-potential table.

**Work:** Reconstruct one metal/aqueous equilibrium calculation from activities, temperature and a stated reference. Explain how pH, complexation or a changed gas condition moves a boundary. Distinguish an equilibrium potential–pH map from a corrosion-rate map. Show why thermodynamic permission neither predicts a localized attack site nor establishes the time until loss of function. Return to oxidation-state bookkeeping when the charge or reaction extent is ambiguous.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
